  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\n- Small Chance of Rain Saturday; Soaking Rain Again Sunday\n\nLatest observations this afternoon indicate that the snow potential \nhas just about ended for this event across the area. Just about all \nof the observations are now reporting rain where it is \nprecipitating. Overall, things worked out fairly well, with the \nmesoscale fgen band ending up just a tad further NW than was \nexpected, bringing the light snow accumulations from South Haven, to \nGR, and to just south of Mt. Pleasant. The back edge of the rain is \nnow progressing steadily to the SE, and should end by mid-evening \nfor all locations.\n\nWe continue to expect a short lived dry period tonight, before we \nsee only a chance of some rain showers late Sat morning through mid \nSaturday afternoon. This is associated with a weak short wave, with \nlimited moisture available. Cross sections of the moisture profiles \nshow mid level moisture around during the morning hours. By the time \nthe low levels saturate enough, the mid level moisture is gone. So \nthere is never any really good deep moisture. The best chance for a \nlight shower will be up north closer to the wave.\n\nWe get back to the recent pattern on Sunday where the strong \nsouthern stream waves dive over the Desert SW, and then lift NE \ntoward the Great Lakes. The energy of interest is just coming \nonshore over Oregon this afternoon. It will dig into the southern \nPlains, amplify, and then lift toward the area. This track will \nadvect another good surge of warm and moist air from the Gulf, and \nbring another period of soaking rain to the area.\n\nThis system being of origin with the southern jet, will initially not \nhave any sufficient cold air to bring any snow with it. As the wave \nmoves east of the state, there will be some phasing with a north \nstream wave that eventually brings enough colder air in for snow. \nThere has been good agreement amongst the models and ensemble \nmembers, and run to run consistency, that we are looking at just \nrain for our area before the precipitation ends Sunday night. \n\n- Mainly Dry Monday through Wednesday\n\nWe will see a nice break in the unsettled weather from Monday \nthrough Wednesday of this week. There is a break in the southern \nstream energy, and a large high amplitude upper ridge builds in \nslowly. We do see a warm front that will pass through Tuesday night \nas return flow increases, and brings the warmer air in. The air will \nstill be fairly dry from the large ridge, and the best forcing with \na low level jet will be north of the forecast area. \n\n- Another Wet System Likely Late Next Week\n\nAfter the break in wet weather, the area is poised to once again be \naffected by one of the warm and moist southern stream systems as \nlate next week. It does look like we see a northern stream cold \nfront move south through the area Wednesday night. Then the low that \nis forecast to dig south to Southern California and Baja Mexico will \nbecome negatively tilted and lift NE toward the area. The northern \nstream front will not have much cold air with it. Then the southern \nlow warms things back up, and should bring more rain. Some colder \nair comes in after this system, but it looks to be too late at this \ntime for any impactful snow. This will need to be watched as better \nphasing could make things more interesting.",
